.\" Manual page for safexec 1.9.6.
.\" Written from safexec.c, the Makefile, and the test suite.
.\"
.TH SAFEXEC 1 2026-09-29 "safexec 1.9.6"
.SH NAME
safexec \- run allowlisted tools with dropped privileges
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY safexec
.RI [ wrapper " ...]"
.I tool
.RI [ argument " ...]"
.YS
.SY safexec
.BI \-\-kill= pid
.YS
.SY safexec
.BR \-\-help " | " \-h
.YS
.SY safexec
.BR \-\-version " | " \-v
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B safexec
runs a fixed set of command-line tools
on behalf of a less-trusted caller,
such as a web application, a scheduler, or a job runner.
It is intended to be installed set-user-ID root.
For each request it performs these steps:
.IP (1) 4
The tool is matched by basename against a compile-time allowlist.
.IP (2)
The tool, and every wrapper given by bare name,
is resolved to an absolute pathname
under a fixed set of trusted directories.
The caller's
.B PATH
and working directory are never consulted.
.IP (3)
When the effective user ID is 0,
the environment is cleared,
the process joins a new cgroup v2 group
(or, failing that, has resource limits applied),
and the process state is hardened.
.IP (4)
Privileges are dropped,
the no-new-privileges flag is set,
inherited file descriptors are closed,
and the tool is executed.
.P
.B safexec
never starts a shell and never runs a tool as root.
Arguments after the tool name reach the tool unchanged,
so shell metacharacters in an argument are passed to the tool literally.
Because
.B safexec
replaces itself with the tool,
the process ID and the exit status seen by the caller
are those of the tool.
.SS Allowlist
The tool is the first argument whose basename names an allowlisted program.
Any directory part of that argument is discarded
and the name is resolved again as described under
.IR "Path pinning" ;
for example,
.I /tmp/evil/curl
runs the trusted
.BR curl (1)
and never the file at that path.
A program that is on the allowlist but not installed
is refused with the message
.IR "cannot resolve trusted path" .
.P
The following programs are always available:
.TP 12
.B Search
.BR rg (1)
.TP
.B Fetch
.BR wget (1),
.BR curl (1)
.TP
.B Archives
.BR tar (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR gunzip (1),
.BR xz (1),
.BR unxz (1),
.BR zip (1),
.BR unzip (1)
.TP
.B Checksums
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR sha512sum (1),
.BR shasum (1),
.BR b2sum (1),
.BR cksum (1)
.TP
.B Media
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1),
.BR magick (1),
.BR convert (1),
.BR identify (1)
.TP
.B Documents
.BR wkhtmltopdf (1),
.BR pdftk (1),
.BR pandoc (1)
.P
The following groups are added only when
.B safexec
is compiled with the corresponding macro
(see
.IR Building ):
.TP
.B SAFEXEC_WITH_GS
.BR gs (1)
.TP
.B SAFEXEC_WITH_POPPLER
.BR pdfinfo (1),
.BR pdftoppm (1),
.BR pdftocairo (1)
.TP
.B SAFEXEC_WITH_DB
.BR mysqldump (1),
.BR mysql (1),
.BR mariadb\-dump (1),
.BR mariadb (1),
.BR pg_dump (1),
.BR pg_restore (1),
.BR psql (1),
.BR redis\-cli (1)
.TP
.B SAFEXEC_WITH_RSYNC_GIT
.BR rsync (1),
.BR git (1)
.P
The shells
.BR sh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR dash ,
.BR ash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR ksh ,
and
.B fish
are never accepted, whether they appear before the tool or as the tool.
.SS Prelude
The arguments that precede the tool form the prelude.
The prelude is scanned from left to right.
Each argument must be one of the following;
the first argument that is none of them ends the prelude,
is taken to be the intended tool,
and is refused unless it is allowlisted.
.TP
.B Wrapper
One of
.BR nohup ,
.BR nice ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR stdbuf ,
.BR ionice ,
.BR taskset ,
.BR setsid ,
.BR chrt ,
or
.BR time .
A bare name is resolved to a trusted pathname in the same way as the tool.
An explicit pathname must consist of a trusted directory,
a slash,
and the wrapper name, with nothing in between.
Thus
.I /usr/bin/nice
is accepted, while
.IR /usr/bin/../bin/nice ,
.IR /usr/bin/x/nice ,
.IR ./nice ,
and
.I /tmp/nice
are refused.
The named file must be executable and must be either a regular file
or a symbolic link whose real path is a regular file
under a trusted directory.
.TP
.B Option
An argument that begins with a hyphen and has at least one further character,
or the argument
.BR \-\- .
Options are not interpreted, but an option that contains a slash is refused,
so that a construct such as
.I "timeout 5 \-dir/prog"
cannot name a program.
.TP
.B Number or value
A signed decimal integer;
a hexadecimal number beginning with
.BR 0x ;
or a string that begins with a digit and continues with digits,
periods, commas, and hyphens,
optionally followed by one of the suffixes
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h ,
or
.BR d .
These forms cover durations
.RB ( 5 ,
.BR 1.5 ,
.BR 2m )
and CPU lists and masks
.RB ( 0x3 ,
.BR 0,1 ,
.BR 0\-3 ).
.IP
Wrapper options that take their value as a separate word
are not recognized unless that word has one of these forms.
Write
.IR "timeout \-sKILL 20" ,
not
.IR "timeout \-s KILL 20" .
.TP
.B Proxy assignment
An argument of the form
.IB NAME = VALUE
where
.I NAME
is
.BR HTTP_PROXY ,
.BR HTTPS_PROXY ,
.BR ALL_PROXY ,
.BR NO_PROXY ,
or the lowercase form of one of these.
At most 16 assignments are accepted.
They may appear at any position in the prelude.
Assignments are removed from the argument vector
and exported to the environment of the tool.
Every other assignment is refused,
including
.BR PATH ,
.BR IFS ,
.BR LD_* ,
.BR DYLD_* ,
.BR PYTHONPATH ,
.BR PERL5OPT ,
.BR RUBYOPT ,
.BR GEM_HOME ,
.BR GEM_PATH ,
.BR GCONV_PATH ,
.BR LOCPATH ,
.BR TZDIR ,
.BR MALLOC_CONF ,
and any unknown name.
.SS Path pinning
The tool and each bare-name wrapper are looked up in the following
trusted directories, in this order:
.IP
.I /usr/bin
.br
.I /bin
.br
.I /usr/local/bin
.br
.I /usr/sbin
.br
.I /sbin
.br
.I /usr/local/sbin
.br
.I /run/current-system/sw/bin
.br
.I /usr/pkg/bin
.br
.I /usr/pkg/sbin
.P
On macOS,
.IR /opt/homebrew/bin ,
.IR /opt/homebrew/sbin ,
.IR /opt/local/bin ,
and
.I /opt/local/sbin
are also trusted.
The first directory that holds an executable candidate wins.
.P
A regular file is accepted directly.
A symbolic link is accepted only if its real path is an executable regular file
that itself lies under a trusted directory.
In that case the pathname of the link, not the real path, is executed,
so that multi-call binaries such as BusyBox and coreutils
receive the correct
.IR argv[0] .
A link that resolves outside the trusted directories is skipped
and the search continues.
For example, on Debian
.BR pg_dump ,
.BR pg_restore ,
and
.B psql
are links to
.IR \%/usr/share/postgresql\-common/pg_wrapper ,
which is outside the trusted directories,
so they are refused.
.P
Trust is decided by location only.
.B safexec
does not check the owner or mode of the resolved file.
.SS Privileged mode
Privileged mode applies whenever the effective user ID is 0:
when the binary is installed set-user-ID root,
and also when it is run by root.
In this mode
.B safexec
performs the following steps in order:
.IP (1) 5
The environment is cleared and rebuilt as described under
.IR "Environment of the tool" .
Proxy assignments from the prelude are then exported.
.IP (2)
The umask is set to 077 and the process is marked non-dumpable
.RB ( PR_SET_DUMPABLE
set to 0).
.IP (3)
The tool is isolated (see
.IR Isolation ).
.IP (4)
The target user is chosen:
.B nobody
for every tool except
.BR rg ,
and the owner of the searched directory for
.B rg
(see
.IR rg ).
.IP (5)
Supplementary groups are cleared,
and the group ID and user ID are set to those of the target user.
.IP (6)
If the drop fails,
or if the user
.B nobody
does not exist,
.B safexec
falls back to the real user ID and group ID of the caller,
again with supplementary groups cleared.
If the effective user ID is still 0 after that,
.B safexec
prints a fatal error and exits with status 3.
A tool is never executed as root.
.IP (7)
The working directory is checked
(see
.BR SAFEXEC_SAFE_CWD ),
and the
.B wget
directory argument is rewritten if necessary (see
.IR wget ).
.IP (8)
On Linux,
.B PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
is set.
.IP (9)
A summary is written to standard error (see
.IR Diagnostics ).
.IP (10)
All file descriptors above 2 are closed.
.IP (11)
The tool is executed.
.SS Pass-through mode
When the effective user ID is not 0,
because the binary lacks the set-user-ID bit,
resides on a
.B nosuid
filesystem,
or was copied without its mode,
only the allowlist, the prelude checks, and path pinning apply.
The tool is then executed as the invoking user.
The environment is not sanitized,
privileges are not changed,
no isolation is set up,
no-new-privileges is not set,
inherited file descriptors stay open,
and the checks specific to
.B rg
and
.B wget
are not performed.
Proxy assignments from the prelude are still exported,
and
.B SAFEXEC_SAFE_CWD
is still honored.
A notice is written to standard error.
.SS Environment of the tool
In privileged mode the caller's environment is discarded completely.
The tool receives only:
.TP
.B PATH
.I \%/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/run/current\-system/sw/bin
.TP
.BR LANG ", " LC_CTYPE
.B C.UTF\-8
if that locale can be selected,
otherwise
.BR en_US.UTF\-8 ,
otherwise
.BR C .
.TP
.B CHARSET
.B UTF\-8
for the two UTF-8 locales,
.B ASCII
for
.BR C .
.TP
Proxy variables
Those given as proxy assignments in the prelude.
.P
In pass-through mode the tool inherits the caller's environment,
plus any proxy assignments.
.SS Isolation
In privileged mode
.B safexec
isolates the tool before it drops privileges.
The method is selected by
.BR SAFEXEC_DETACH .
.P
With cgroup v2,
the process is moved into a new group named
.BI safexec\-run. pid
below
.IR "<cgroup root>" /safexec\-run,
which is created if necessary.
.I <cgroup root>
is
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
if
.I cgroup.controllers
is readable there,
otherwise
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup/unified .
The controllers
.BR memory ,
.BR pids ,
.BR cpu ,
.BR io ,
and
.B cpuset
are enabled where the kernel permits.
Failure to enable a controller is not an error.
Because the group is created by the process that then becomes the tool,
.B safexec
cannot remove it on exit;
empty groups are removed by later invocations.
.P
With the rlimits fallback,
.B safexec
applies resource limits for address space, CPU time, open files,
and process count,
and optionally adjusts the nice value and I/O priority.
.P
The limits are compiled in
(see
.B safexec_default_limits()
in the source).
As shipped, none of them imposes a ceiling,
so isolation groups and tracks the tool process
but does not restrict its CPU, memory, or I/O use
unless the source is modified and rebuilt.
.SS rg
In privileged mode
.BR rg (1)
runs as the owner of the directory it searches,
so that it can read exactly what that user can read.
The last argument is examined.
It must:
.IP \[bu] 3
begin with a slash;
.IP \[bu]
name a directory that is not a symbolic link; and
.IP \[bu]
name a directory that is not owned by root.
.P
If the owner is the invoking user,
the tool runs as that user,
with the real user ID and real group ID of the caller
and supplementary groups cleared.
Otherwise the owner must exist in the password database,
and
.B safexec
sets the user ID and the primary group ID of that user,
with supplementary groups cleared.
If any condition fails,
.B safexec
exits with status 3.
.P
Only the last argument is examined,
and only in privileged mode.
.SS wget
If the arguments contain
.B \-P
followed by
.I /tmp
as two separate arguments,
and
.I /tmp
is not writable by the user the tool will run as,
the second argument is replaced by
.IR /tmp/safexec\-work/ uid ,
where
.I uid
is the effective user ID.
Forms such as
.B \-P/tmp
and
.B \-\-directory\-prefix=/tmp
are not rewritten.
.P
In privileged mode
.B safexec
creates
.I /tmp/safexec\-work
if it does not exist,
owned by root with mode 1777.
If it already exists it must be a directory,
not a symbolic link,
and owned by root,
or the fallback is not used.
The per-user subdirectory is created with mode 0700
and must belong to the target user.
If any of these conditions cannot be met,
.B safexec
prints a warning and leaves
.B \-P /tmp
unchanged.
.SS Kill mode
.B safexec \-\-kill=\c
.I pid
sends
.B SIGTERM
to a process that
.B safexec
started.
The request is refused with exit status 1 unless all of the following hold:
.IP \[bu] 3
.I pid
is a decimal number from 1 to
.BR INT_MAX ;
.IP \[bu]
the process exists;
.IP \[bu]
the process belongs to the user
.BR nobody ;
and
.IP \[bu]
the process is in a
.I safexec\-run
cgroup or, when it is not (for example, where cgroup delegation
is unavailable),
has
.B NoNewPrivs
set to 1 in
.IR /proc/ pid /status .
.P
The signal is sent with
.BR pidfd_send_signal (2)
when available, which avoids PID reuse races,
and with
.BR kill (2)
otherwise.
On success
.B safexec
prints
.B Success: Killed PID
.I pid
to standard output and exits with status 0.
.P
A process that
.B safexec
started for
.BR rg ,
or that fell back to the invoking user,
does not belong to
.B nobody
and so cannot be stopped this way.
A process owned by
.B nobody
can be signaled only by a privileged sender,
so
.B \-\-kill
must be used through the set-user-ID binary or by root.
Kill mode is available on Linux only.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognized only as the first argument.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit with status 0.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the program name, version, and copyright notice
to standard output and exit with status 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-kill= pid
Send
.B SIGTERM
to the given process
(see
.IR "Kill mode" ).
.P
A first argument that begins with a hyphen and is not one of the above,
a first argument that consists only of digits,
and
.B \-\-kill
without
.BI = pid
are usage errors:
the usage summary is printed and
.B safexec
exits with status 3.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.BR \-\-help ,
.BR \-\-version ,
or a successful
.BR \-\-kill .
.TP
.I "status of the tool"
After the tool has been executed
.B safexec
no longer exists,
so the caller sees the exit status of the tool unchanged.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-kill
failed: invalid PID, nonexistent process, ineligible process,
signal failure, or a non-Linux system.
.TP
.B 3
.B safexec
refused the request or failed before executing the tool.
This covers usage errors;
a tool that is not allowlisted or is a shell;
a tool or wrapper that cannot be resolved to a trusted pathname;
a rejected prelude
(wrapper path outside the trusted directories, path-like option,
disallowed assignment, or more than 16 assignments);
an
.B rg
directory that is missing, relative, not a directory,
a symbolic link, root-owned, or owned by a user absent from the
password database;
isolation that fails when
.B \%SAFEXEC_DETACH
is
.BR cgv2 ;
a privilege drop that leaves the effective user ID at 0;
and a failure of the final
.BR exec .
.P
Status 3 is also a valid exit status of some tools,
and
.BR rg (1)
itself uses 0, 1, and 2.
A caller that must distinguish a refusal from a tool failure
should also inspect standard error,
which
.B SAFEXEC_QUIET
suppresses.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
These variables are read before the environment is cleared.
.TP
.B SAFEXEC_DETACH
Selects the isolation method in privileged mode.
The value is not case-sensitive.
An unrecognized or empty value selects
.BR auto .
.RS
.TP
.B auto
(default)
Use cgroup v2 and fall back to resource limits if it cannot be used.
.TP
.B cgv2
Use cgroup v2 only.
If it is unavailable or the join fails,
.B safexec
exits with status 3.
.TP
.B rlimits
Skip cgroups and apply resource limits.
.TP
.B off
Set up no isolation.
.RE
.IP
On glibc the variable is read with
.BR secure_getenv (3),
so it is ignored when an unprivileged user runs the set-user-ID binary.
Only root, or a caller running a binary that is not set-user-ID,
can change the mode.
On other C libraries it is read with
.BR getenv (3).
.TP
.B SAFEXEC_QUIET
Suppress the diagnostics that
.B safexec
writes,
including errors and the usage summary.
The output of the tool,
the version text,
and the success message of
.B \-\-kill
are not affected.
The values
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
.BR yes ,
and
.B on
enable it;
.BR 0 ,
.BR false ,
.BR no ,
and
.B off
disable it;
matching is not case-sensitive.
Any other value selects the compiled-in default,
which is 0 unless changed with
.BR \-DSAFEXEC_QUIET_DEFAULT .
.TP
.B SAFEXEC_SAFE_CWD
Controls whether
.B safexec
changes to
.I /tmp
(or, if that fails,
.IR / )
when the working directory is not writable.
The check is made after privileges are dropped.
The values
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
.BR yes ,
and
.B on
enable the change unconditionally;
.BR 0 ,
.BR false ,
.BR no ,
and
.B off
disable it.
Any other value, including
.BR \-1 ,
selects the compiled-in default,
which is \-1 unless changed with
.BR \-DSAFEXEC_SAFE_CWD_DEFAULT .
The setting \-1 acts only when standard input, output, or error
is a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/safexec
Default installation path of the binary.
Mode 4755 (owner root) enables privileged mode.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/safexec.1
Default installation path of this manual page.
.TP
.I /sys/fs/cgroup/safexec\-run/
Base cgroup v2 parent, created on demand.
See
.IR Isolation
for the alternative location under
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup/unified .
.TP
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup/safexec\-run/safexec\-run. pid
Per-invocation cgroup v2 group.
.TP
.I /tmp/safexec\-work/
Root-owned, mode 1777 parent of the fallback download directories used for
.BR wget .
.TP
.IR /tmp/safexec\-work/ uid
Per-user download directory, mode 0700.
.TP
.IR /proc/ pid /status ", " /proc/ pid /cgroup
Read by
.B \-\-kill
to check the owner and the isolation of the target.
.TP
.I /proc/self/fd
Read to close inherited file descriptors.
.SH NOTES
.SS Diagnostics
Informational, warning, and error messages are written to standard error
with the prefixes
.BR Info: ,
.BR Warning: ,
.BR Error: ,
.BR Fatal: ,
.BR Note: ,
and
.BR Summary: .
The usage summary,
the version text,
and the success message of
.B \-\-kill
go to standard output.
.B SAFEXEC_QUIET
suppresses the messages on standard error and the usage summary.
It does not suppress the version text or the success message of
.BR \-\-kill .
.P
In privileged mode, just before the tool is executed,
.B safexec
prints the identity it runs under,
the state of no-new-privileges,
and the cgroup or the fact that resource limits are in effect,
followed by the limit values it can read from the cgroup:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
Info: pinned tool \(aqsha256sum\(aq \-> \(aq/usr/bin/sha256sum\(aq
Info: using cgroup v2 child /sys/fs/cgroup/safexec\-run/safexec\-run.\fIpid\fP
Summary: user=65534:65534 (ruid=65534 rgid=65534) cwd=\fI...\fP tool=/usr/bin/sha256sum
Summary: no_new_privs=on
Summary: cgroup=/sys/fs/cgroup/safexec\-run/safexec\-run.\fIpid\fP
.EE
.in
.SS Installation
.B safexec
provides its privilege drop only when installed set-user-ID root:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
chown root:root /usr/local/sbin/safexec
chmod 4755 /usr/local/sbin/safexec
.EE
.in
.P
Do not install it on a
.B nosuid
filesystem.
To restrict who can invoke it,
use mode 4750 and a dedicated group.
The Makefile installs with mode 0755 by default,
so that unprivileged package builds work,
and never changes ownership;
the package must set the set-user-ID bit,
for example with
.BR "make install BINMODE=4755" .
Some packaging tools, such as
.BR \%dh_fixperms
on Debian,
remove the bit and must be told to restore it.
.SS Building
The optional tool groups are enabled with preprocessor macros:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
make EXTRA_CPPFLAGS="\-DSAFEXEC_WITH_POPPLER \-DSAFEXEC_WITH_DB"
.EE
.in
.P
The macros are
.BR SAFEXEC_WITH_GS ,
.BR SAFEXEC_WITH_POPPLER ,
.BR SAFEXEC_WITH_DB ,
and
.BR SAFEXEC_WITH_RSYNC_GIT .
.B gs
has a long history of sandbox-escape vulnerabilities,
and
.B rsync
and
.B git
can start
.BR ssh (1),
which lies outside the closed set of tools the allowlist assumes.
Enable these groups only if you control how the tools are invoked.
.P
.B \-DSAFEXEC_QUIET_DEFAULT
and
.B \-DSAFEXEC_SAFE_CWD_DEFAULT
set the compiled-in defaults of the corresponding variables.
Statically linked musl binaries do not load NSS modules,
so users provided by LDAP or SSSD are not resolved;
this affects the owner lookup for
.BR rg .
.SS Platforms
Linux supports the full feature set.
Other POSIX systems get the allowlist, path pinning, resource limits,
and descriptor closing,
but no cgroups,
no
.BR pidfd_send_signal (2),
and no
.BR \-\-kill .
.SS Testing
.B make check
runs a smoke test that needs no root privileges;
it skips the privilege-drop test unless run as root
against a set-user-ID root binary.
A separate end-to-end suite,
.IR tests/run\-all.sh ,
modifies the host by installing packages,
adding users,
and installing set-user-ID root binaries.
It refuses to run unless
.B SAFEXEC_TEST_CONTAINER=1
is set and must be run only in a disposable container or virtual machine.
.SH CAVEATS
.B safexec
is not a general-purpose sandbox.
It constrains the process it starts and its descendants,
and it permits only allowlisted tools,
but it does not filter the arguments of those tools.
Options such as
.B "rg \-\-pre"
and
.B "wget \-\-execute"
make the tool run programs or modify files
with the privileges of the target user.
Expose the set-user-ID binary only to callers that are trusted with this.
.P
In pass-through mode none of the protections beyond the allowlist
and path pinning are in effect.
.P
A caller that may run
.B safexec
may run
.B rg
over any directory that is not owned by root,
with the privileges of the owner of that directory.
.P
Any caller that may run
.B safexec
may send
.B SIGTERM
to any eligible process with
.BR \-\-kill ;
the caller is not required to have started the process.
.P
Tools run as
.BR nobody ,
so files they create belong to
.BR nobody ,
and files that they are to read must be readable by that user.
The caller's environment,
including proxy variables,
does not reach the tool;
use proxy assignments in the prelude instead.
.P
Trust in the resolved pathname rests on its directory alone.
The trusted directories must not be writable by untrusted users.
.SH BUGS
Wrapper options that take a value as a separate word,
such as
.IR "timeout \-s KILL" ,
are refused.
.P
The
.B wget
directory rewrite recognizes only the two-argument form
.BR "\-P /tmp" .
.P
Programs that are links to targets outside the trusted directories,
such as the PostgreSQL clients on Debian,
cannot be used.
.SH EXAMPLES
Fetch a page:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
safexec curl \-fsS \-o /var/tmp/page.html https://example.com/
.EE
.in
.P
Use a wrapper chain and a proxy variable:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
safexec HTTP_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:3128 nice \-n 10 timeout 30 \e
        curl \-fsSL https://example.com/
.EE
.in
.P
Compute a checksum:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
safexec sha256sum /srv/data/archive.tar.gz
.EE
.in
.P
Search a project directory as the user that owns it:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
safexec rg \-m 1 \-\-text \(aqpattern\(aq /home/alice/project
.EE
.in
.P
Start a job in the background and stop it later.
The process ID stays the same because
.B safexec
replaces itself with the tool:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
safexec ffmpeg \-v error \-i in.mp4 out.webm &
pid=$!
safexec \-\-kill=$pid
.EE
.in
.P
Requests that are refused:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
$ \fBsafexec ls /\fP
Error: \(aqls\(aq is not allowed by safexec.
$ \fBecho $?\fP
3
$ \fBsafexec /bin/sh \-c id\fP
Info: rejecting shell interpreter before tool: \(aq/bin/sh\(aq
Error: \(aqsh\(aq is not allowed by safexec.
.EE
.in
.P
Suppress diagnostics and rely on the exit status:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
SAFEXEC_QUIET=1 safexec sha256sum /srv/data/archive.tar.gz
.EE
.in
.SH REPORTING BUGS
Report bugs through
.UR https://github.com/psaux-it/safexec
the safexec repository on GitHub
